modem73 is Unlicense software by RFnexus:
https://github.com/RFnexus/modem73

Windows binaries come from the experimental Win32 port:
https://github.com/RFnexus/modem73-win

Release archives include a modem73 built from the upstream source at a
pinned tag (.github/workflows/modem73.yml) with a patch added:
`patches/network-audio.patch` (`patches/network-audio-win.patch` for the
Windows port). It adds network audio (`-d tcp:HOST:PORT`), which WeeChat
Radio uses to carry a Bluetooth radio's audio (VR-N76 / VR-N7600) into
modem73 with no sound card. The Linux and macOS patch also adds the
`audio_in_db` level to the control port's status and a `get_spectrum`
command, which feed the level line and the waterfall on `wcr web`'s page.
Linux and macOS builds also get `patches/encode-before-busy-test.patch`:
modem73 encodes a frame to audio before its CSMA busy test instead of
after `CSMA: Channel clear`, so PTT keys straight after the clear (on a
Pi 3 the encode took about 1 s, long enough for another station to key
in that gap). It logs `TX: frame encoded before the busy test (N ms)`.
Both patches are offered upstream; once
upstream ships network audio the patch goes away. The patch is under the
same Unlicense as modem73. That binary is not Apache-2.0.
macOS archives also include the `libs/` folder (`@executable_path/libs`).
See the Unlicense at the upstream repository.

`wcr` downloads a missing modem73 from its **own** WeeChat Radio release
tag, never from `/releases/latest`. The release workflow hashes the modem73
asset it bundles and builds that SHA-256 into `wcr` (`WCR_MODEM73_SHA256`),
so the download always matches the bundled copy. Builds made outside the
release workflow fall back to release `v0.1.79` and the hashes in
`sha256.json` next to this file.
